84 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 65 0 R
/E (Growing up in Louisiana, I have always placed family at the center of my values. I was born and raised in Baton Rouge, where I first learned that the strength of a community begins with the strength of its families. It is also where, working in women's health, I was appalled by the rates of maternal and infant loss in the place I love. These were never just numbers to me. They were my neighbors, my sisters. I felt a need I could not ignore, and one question drove me: how could I make this better in my community?)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
85 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 65 0 R
/E (The answer starts with the mother herself. We do not rescue mothers. We empower them. When a woman feels informed, supported, and seen during her pregnancy, she makes bigger, braver decisions for herself and her child. You do not change one life. You change two. That conviction became Mary's Hands Network.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
86 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 65 0 R
/E (I have seen what becomes possible. A nineteen-year-old came to us pregnant for the first time, living in a group home with no parents and no partner. With her doula by her side, she built a nest of support for herself and her daughter. She delivered a healthy baby girl and went from homeless to housed, enrolled in college with a career ahead. We did not save her. She saved herself. We made sure she was never alone while she was doing it.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
87 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 65 0 R
/E (And mothers come back to us. Three have returned to walk with us through a second baby. We have stood beside five different mothers carrying twins, supported mothers through vaginal births after cesareans, and watched women we once served come back to train as doulas while doulas return to be cared for as mothers. In three years, we have supported more than 500 families, and among the mothers we serve, cesarean births are nearly 60 percent lower than the Louisiana average, preterm births are more than 75 percent lower, and low birth weight is nearly 80 percent lower.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
88 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 65 0 R
/E (Every one of those mothers was met by a doula in a lavender jacket. More than 260 women now wear it, and that jacket has become our promise: that no mother in our community will face her pregnancy alone. It is our legacy in lavender, stitched one mother and one doula at a time.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
89 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 65 0 R
/E (When you sponsor the In Her Honor Awards Gala: A Legacy in Lavender, you place another lavender jacket beside another mother. Support at every level leaves a mark, and together those marks become a legacy that outlasts us all. If you want to change the world, start where the world begins, with a mother and her child. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
